{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, if you surely hear.</b> Rashi explained: if you hear in the old, you will hear in the new, for so the Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Avot 4:2) — the reward of a commandment is a commandment, and hearing draws after hearing, that is, from the old to the new. And as for what is stated, 'with all your heart and with all your soul,' and 'with all your me'od' is not stated here — according to what Rashi explained, that 'with all your me'od' means: with all your wealth, for there are some people for whom their wealth is more precious to them than their body — this is not commonly found except in one person or a few individuals whose mind is negated before every person, but not in the public, for it is not common for the many to agree upon this defective opinion. And according to what Rashi explained, 'with all your me'od' means with every measure that He measures out to you, whether for bad or for good — behold, this passage speaks of the time of receiving reward, and that is a time of love, and the mention of blessing upon evil has no place here, for it is not according to the time in which one stands. And further, it appears that there is no need to say here that one should serve YHWH with every measure that He measures out to him, even in a time of receiving evil, for this matter is already stated explicitly in this passage, as it says: 'And you shall perish quickly from off the land, etc. — therefore you shall place these words of Mine upon your heart' (Deuteronomy 11:17–18), meaning that even in a time when they are perishing from every direction, they should serve YHWH in any case and place His words upon their heart. And so Rashi explained (ad loc. v. 18). And if so, this matter is stated explicitly; therefore there was no need to say 'and with all your me'od.'" "And the passage is stated in plural language, except for 'you shall gather your grain,' for so it is stated (Exodus 23:25): 'And you shall serve YHWH your Elohim, and He will bless your bread,' which the early commentators explained — for the service of YHWH is better in a gathering, as it is written (Job 36:5): 'Behold, El is mighty, and does not despise' — but eating is better when each person eats his bread alone, and he should not multiply meals with companions. Therefore it is stated here: 'and to serve Him with all your heart, etc. — and you shall gather your grain,' for it is better when each person gathers his grain for himself, and likewise when he sits in his house alone, as it is written: 'when you sit in your house and when you walk on the way and when you lie down and when you rise' (Deuteronomy 6:7) — for all these things are not fitting to be said about the collective."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:01.313654453Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}